get your job done better.

00:00:30.650 --> 00:00:32.150
But if you're new
to the frameworks,

00:00:32.150 --> 00:00:36.310
the next couple of slides will
give you a quick introduction.

00:00:36.310 --> 00:00:38.770
So the first thing
to understand is

00:00:38.770 --> 00:00:40.720
that Scikit-Learn
and XGBoost are

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:43.510
part of a larger Python
ecosystem for data science

00:00:43.510 --> 00:00:45.640
which is incredibly rich.

00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:49.930
From low-level libraries like
numpy and pandas all the way up

00:00:49.930 --> 00:00:53.050
through graphing and
matplotlib and the frameworks

00:00:53.050 --> 00:00:54.700
we're talking about today.

00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:59.800
Python has a ton of frameworks
to make data science easy.

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:02.800
Python was the
most preferred tool

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:09.030
cited in Kaggle's most recent
ML and data science survey.

00:01:09.030 --> 00:01:12.300
Scikit-Learn is an
open-source framework

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:15.960
on Python for doing a
wide variety of ML tasks.

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:17.940
The name Scikit-Learn
is derived from it

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:21.270
being a toolkit for
machine learning

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:24.430
that's part of the
scientific Python community.

00:01:24.430 --> 00:01:26.010
And I have to say,
I'm personally

00:01:26.010 --> 00:01:28.710
thrilled to be here
today with David.

00:01:28.710 --> 00:01:32.010
In 2007, David
created Scikit-Learn

00:01:32.010 --> 00:01:35.600
as part of a Google
Summer of Code project.

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:38.980
Scikit-Learn is, by the way,
often abbreviated SK-Learn.

00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:44.630
And I think you'll hear us use
both terms interchangeably.

00:01:44.630 --> 00:01:47.290
So unlike Scikit-Learn, which
supports dozens and dozens

00:01:47.290 --> 00:01:52.150
of algorithms, XGBoost supports
one, but does a fantastic job

00:01:52.150 --> 00:01:53.170
with it.

00:01:53.170 --> 00:01:58.090
So basically, XGBoost stands
for Extreme Gradient Boosted

00:01:58.090 --> 00:01:59.560
decision trees.

00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:01.210
So gradient boosted
decision trees

00:02:01.210 --> 00:02:05.350
are ensembles of decision trees
in which each subsequent tree

00:02:05.350 --> 00:02:08.949
attempts to correct the mistakes
made by the prior trees.

00:02:08.949 --> 00:02:11.800
And it's proven to be an
incredibly effective method

00:02:11.800 --> 00:02:13.480
of doing ML.

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:17.230
I was talking with Ben Hamner,
who is the CTO of Kaggle.

00:02:17.230 --> 00:02:19.750
And he told me that
XGBoost is behind almost

00:02:19.750 --> 00:02:24.830
every recent winning submission
to a Kaggle competition,

00:02:24.830 --> 00:02:28.520
except for computer
vision competitions.

00:02:28.520 --> 00:02:30.570
And that brings me to
an important point.

00:02:30.570 --> 00:02:33.410
So neither XGBoost
nor SK-Learn is

00:02:33.410 --> 00:02:36.740
optimized around deep
learning or neural networks.

00:02:36.740 --> 00:02:39.710
And that's a key
difference from TensorFlow.

00:02:39.710 --> 00:02:41.240
Now, you're at a
Google conference,

00:02:41.240 --> 00:02:42.990
and you're hearing a
lot about TensorFlow.

00:02:42.990 --> 00:02:44.810
So I just want to
take a moment here.

00:02:44.810 --> 00:02:48.800
TensorFlow is truly remarkable,
and you should definitely

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:50.690
go and learn a lot about it.

00:02:50.690 --> 00:02:53.340
But for a class of
problems, and, I think,

00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:55.340
for a class of problems
that happens to actually

00:02:55.340 --> 00:02:59.890
be very common in
businesses, I think the ROI

00:02:59.890 --> 00:03:04.160
from XGBoost and SK-Learn can
be higher than TensorFlow.

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:08.080
You'll get great results
without a ton of effort.

00:03:10.670 --> 00:03:12.970
So we're going to focus
on three stages of ML

00:03:12.970 --> 00:03:15.040
during today's presentation.

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:16.690
Preprocessing is the
stage in which you

00:03:16.690 --> 00:03:19.010
make data ready for training.

00:03:19.010 --> 00:03:21.340
Training is the stage
in which you take labels

00:03:21.340 --> 00:03:24.490
data, train on it so that
you can predict a target

00:03:24.490 --> 00:03:27.852
variable in the future
for unlabelled data.

00:03:27.852 --> 00:03:29.560
And then prediction
is the stage in which

00:03:29.560 --> 00:03:33.625
you take unlabelled data and
predict new values for it.

00:03:33.625 --> 00:03:40.180
And how do these frameworks
play into these three phases?

00:03:40.180 --> 00:03:43.350
Preprocessing often requires
transformations in NumPy

00:03:43.350 --> 00:03:44.410
or pandas.

00:03:44.410 --> 00:03:47.020
But Scikit-Learn also
provides its own libraries

00:03:47.020 --> 00:03:48.490
for preprocessing.

00:03:48.490 --> 00:03:52.090
You can do feature selection
and feature engineering

00:03:52.090 --> 00:03:54.445
with Scikit-Learn itself.

00:03:54.445 --> 00:03:56.320
And one of the best
things about Scikit-Learn

00:03:56.320 --> 00:03:58.510
is the consistency
of its interface.

00:03:58.510 --> 00:04:02.680
So you can take any algorithm
in Scikit-Learn and call .fit

00:04:02.680 --> 00:04:05.320
on it and get a trained model.

00:04:05.320 --> 00:04:07.060
And once you have
a trained model,

00:04:07.060 --> 00:04:10.210
you can take any of them and
call .predict on it and get

00:04:10.210 --> 00:04:11.920
predictions out.

00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:14.340
This interface is
incredibly powerful.

00:04:14.340 --> 00:04:16.630
And I think it's what makes
Scikit-Learn a ton of fun

00:04:16.630 --> 00:04:18.230
to use.

00:04:18.230 --> 00:04:22.089
XGBoost supports
that same interface,

00:04:22.089 --> 00:04:23.350
which is really cool.

00:04:23.350 --> 00:04:27.070
So if you built your
pipeline to use Scikit-Learn,

00:04:27.070 --> 00:04:32.357
you can often switch XGBoost
into it pretty seamlessly.

00:04:32.357 --> 00:04:33.940
And one of the things
you'll see today

00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:35.481
is that we're going
to do some demos.

00:04:35.481 --> 00:04:37.940
The demos all use Scikit-Learn
and Scikit-Learn pipelines,

00:04:37.940 --> 00:04:41.230
but you can easily
switch XGBoost into them

00:04:41.230 --> 00:04:43.910
without much effort.

00:04:43.910 --> 00:04:46.760
So let me talk about our agenda
for the rest of the talk.

00:04:46.760 --> 00:04:48.910
We're going to divide it
into three sections based

00:04:48.910 --> 00:04:52.060
on the Google Cloud services
that are demonstrated.

00:04:52.060 --> 00:04:53.800
And during these
three demonstrations,

00:04:53.800 --> 00:04:56.032
we're going to refer back
to those stages of ML

00:04:56.032 --> 00:04:56.990
that I just showed you.

00:04:59.790 --> 00:05:01.970
So in the first case,
what we're going

00:05:01.970 --> 00:05:04.700
to show you is how you
can use Google's building

00:05:04.700 --> 00:05:09.440
blocks APIs to preprocess
unstructured data

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:14.230
so that you can use it in
XGBoost and Scikit-Learn.

00:05:14.230 --> 00:05:15.730
And in the second
phase, we're going

00:05:15.730 --> 00:05:19.180
to talk about Compute
Engine and how it can help

00:05:19.180 --> 00:05:22.690
you do ad-hoc data
analysis at scale

00:05:22.690 --> 00:05:26.230
very easily, and without
the pain and the difficulty

00:05:26.230 --> 00:05:27.484
of distribution.

00:05:27.484 --> 00:05:29.650
I like to refer to that
part of the talk as the part

00:05:29.650 --> 00:05:34.900
where we make distributed
computing uncool again.

00:05:34.900 --> 00:05:36.650
And then we're going
to finish by tackling

00:05:36.650 --> 00:05:40.070
