00:05:42.410 --> 00:05:43.222
reliable way.

00:05:43.222 --> 00:05:44.930
That's the part where
we're going to talk

00:05:44.930 --> 00:05:46.190
about Google Cloud ML Engine.

00:05:49.700 --> 00:05:52.230
So let's dive in.

00:05:52.230 --> 00:05:54.450
So Google's machine
learning building blocks--

00:05:54.450 --> 00:05:57.620
these are pre-trained models
that you can call into.

00:05:57.620 --> 00:06:00.410
And they're accessible by API.

00:06:00.410 --> 00:06:02.210
You basically can
use these models

00:06:02.210 --> 00:06:06.560
to process unstructured data and
utilize Google's deep learning

00:06:06.560 --> 00:06:10.490
expertise without having
to train your own model.

00:06:10.490 --> 00:06:12.800
Building blocks exist for
natural language, speech,

00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:15.380
translation, video, vision--

00:06:15.380 --> 00:06:18.650
and more are coming
at this conference.

00:06:18.650 --> 00:06:22.160
And increasingly, you can
customize these APIs endpoints

00:06:22.160 --> 00:06:24.410
to your business's
needs with auto ML.

00:06:24.410 --> 00:06:26.420
So they're becoming
significantly more powerful

00:06:26.420 --> 00:06:26.961
and flexible.

00:06:29.024 --> 00:06:31.440
I've talked a little bit about
structured and unstructured

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:31.940
data.

00:06:31.940 --> 00:06:34.650
And I just want to quickly
define those concepts.

00:06:34.650 --> 00:06:37.620
Structured data-- essentially
big tables of rows and columns

00:06:37.620 --> 00:06:39.230
of strings and numbers--

00:06:39.230 --> 00:06:40.830
is, in the grand
scheme of things,

00:06:40.830 --> 00:06:43.919
relatively straightforward
to preprocess.

00:06:43.919 --> 00:06:45.210
You can do it with expressions.

00:06:45.210 --> 00:06:48.760
It's not that dissimilar
from spreadsheet operations.

00:06:48.760 --> 00:06:50.280
And if you need to
do it at a scale

00:06:50.280 --> 00:06:54.090
beyond what fits in memory, you
can use things like BigQuery,

00:06:54.090 --> 00:06:57.510
or Spark, or Beam, or
Dataflow to do that.

00:06:57.510 --> 00:06:59.670
But unstructured
data like images,

00:06:59.670 --> 00:07:01.980
long paragraphs
of text, or video,

00:07:01.980 --> 00:07:05.920
is more tricky to preprocess--
more tricky to handle in ML.

00:07:05.920 --> 00:07:07.800
And it's really
where deep learning

00:07:07.800 --> 00:07:09.270
and where neural networks shine.

00:07:09.270 --> 00:07:13.932
And there's a lot of talks
here about those topics.

00:07:13.932 --> 00:07:15.390
But an alternate
path, if you don't

00:07:15.390 --> 00:07:16.973
want to write your
own neural network,

00:07:16.973 --> 00:07:20.700
is to utilize Google's
building blocks APIs.

00:07:20.700 --> 00:07:22.410
So just as an
example, if you have

00:07:22.410 --> 00:07:24.360
a bunch of images
in your business,

00:07:24.360 --> 00:07:26.100
you could train an
algorithm on them

00:07:26.100 --> 00:07:28.740
by extracting the
objects within the images

00:07:28.740 --> 00:07:31.440
and then use that as structured
data in a traditional ML

00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:35.380
approach like
SK-Learn or XGBoost.

00:07:35.380 --> 00:07:38.560
Or here's another
interesting use case.

00:07:38.560 --> 00:07:41.110
It came to us from a
customer engineer in Japan

00:07:41.110 --> 00:07:43.414
whose name is Hayato Yoshikawa.

00:07:43.414 --> 00:07:44.830
I'm also going to
take this moment

00:07:44.830 --> 00:07:46.913
to just say that in this
talk, I will occasionally

00:07:46.913 --> 00:07:48.850
have to mention Japanese
and French names,

00:07:48.850 --> 00:07:50.290
and I'm terrible
at pronunciation,

00:07:50.290 --> 00:07:55.480
and I deeply apologize for
butchering these languages.

00:07:55.480 --> 00:07:57.610
Hayato showed that
it's possible to use

00:07:57.610 --> 00:08:00.430
Google's natural language
API to solve a tricky problem

00:08:00.430 --> 00:08:02.510
with Japanese text.

00:08:02.510 --> 00:08:05.020
So most NLP, most Natural
Language Processing,

00:08:05.020 --> 00:08:08.410
starts with extracting
the words that

00:08:08.410 --> 00:08:11.230
are in a sentence or
a paragraph of text.

00:08:11.230 --> 00:08:12.800
And in English, this is easy.

00:08:12.800 --> 00:08:15.491
You just split the
text on the spaces.

00:08:15.491 --> 00:08:17.740
But in East Asian languages,
it can be more difficult,

00:08:17.740 --> 00:08:21.140
because there aren't
spaces between the words.

00:08:21.140 --> 00:08:23.350
So what Hayato
showed is that you

00:08:23.350 --> 00:08:26.230
can pass the sentences
to Google's NL API

00:08:26.230 --> 00:08:29.050
and extract the tokens out.

00:08:29.050 --> 00:08:31.180
The results he saw
were much better

00:08:31.180 --> 00:08:33.520
than you get from
Python libraries.

00:08:33.520 --> 00:08:35.020
And with the tokenized
text in hand,

00:08:35.020 --> 00:08:38.140
you can pass it to Google,
to Scikit-Learn's text

00:08:38.140 --> 00:08:41.409
preprocessor, and
go on your way.

00:08:41.409 --> 00:08:43.130
So let's see this in action.

00:08:43.130 --> 00:08:45.039
I'm going to switch
over to my demo box.

00:08:45.039 --> 00:08:46.080
It will take me a second.

00:08:51.840 --> 00:08:52.460
Awesome.

00:08:52.460 --> 00:08:53.910
OK.

00:08:53.910 --> 00:08:56.897
So the demos today are
in Jupyter Notebooks.

00:08:56.897 --> 00:08:58.980
And if you're not familiar
with Jupyter Notebooks,

00:08:58.980 --> 00:09:00.750
I really suggest you
take a look at them.

00:09:00.750 --> 00:09:02.930
They're a ton of fun to use.

00:09:02.930 --> 00:09:05.110
They're like an advanced
version of a REPL,

00:09:05.110 --> 00:09:08.390
a Read-Eval-Print-Loop,
where you can execute code,

00:09:08.390 --> 00:09:13.405
mix and match it, and intermix
documentation with code.

00:09:13.405 --> 00:09:15.030
So in this demo, what
we're going to do

00:09:15.030 --> 00:09:18.610
is we're going to preprocess
headlines from "Hacker News."

00:09:18.610 --> 00:09:21.120
It's an open data
set on BigQuery.

00:09:21.120 --> 00:09:24.390
And we're going to try to figure
out whether the headline is

00:09:24.390 --> 00:09:26.790
from "Tech Crunch" or
from "The New York Times"

00:09:26.790 --> 00:09:31.290
by identifying the most
salient words in the headline.

00:09:31.290 --> 00:09:33.600
Let me talk real quickly
about the dependencies here.

00:09:33.600 --> 00:09:37.050
Basic stuff like
pandas and NumPy.

00:09:37.050 --> 00:09:38.590
And then-- whoops.

00:09:38.590 --> 00:09:39.090
Excuse me.

00:09:39.090 --> 00:09:40.860
I'm on my second demo.

00:09:40.860 --> 00:09:45.510
All right, basic stuff
like pandas and not numpy

00:09:45.510 --> 00:09:50.610
as well as text preprocessors
from Scikit-Learn and two

00:09:50.610 --> 00:09:53.190
Google client libraries.

00:09:53.190 --> 00:09:56.400
So the first here reads
data from BigQuery.

00:09:56.400 --> 00:09:59.850
And then the second
here communicates

00:09:59.850 --> 00:10:02.830
with the natural language API.

00:10:02.830 --> 00:10:05.340
So let's run this
line of code here.

00:10:09.230 --> 00:10:10.145
And you know what?

00:10:10.145 --> 00:10:12.020
I'm going to restart my
kernel so that I just

00:10:12.020 --> 00:10:14.252
clear all the output.

00:10:14.252 --> 00:10:15.710
I don't want to
spoil the surprise.

00:10:15.710 --> 00:10:16.892
All right.

00:10:16.892 --> 00:10:17.850
Let's start this again.

00:10:22.040 --> 00:10:22.540
Perfect.

00:10:22.540 --> 00:10:23.998
So we've imported
our dependencies.

00:10:23.998 --> 00:10:28.090
And now we're going to query
the data from BigQuery.

00:10:28.090 --> 00:10:30.340
This is something I really
love about Notebooks.

00:10:30.340 --> 00:10:33.124
You can mix and match, actually,
different forms of code.

00:10:33.124 --> 00:10:34.540
So in this Notebook,
for instance,

00:10:34.540 --> 00:10:38.420
we're mixing Python code
with these BigQuery magics.

00:10:38.420 --> 00:10:40.220
Magics are add-ons
you can install

00:10:40.220 --> 00:10:43.450
onto Notebook environments
to do particular things.

00:10:43.450 --> 00:10:45.310
In this case, the
BigQuery magic allows

00:10:45.310 --> 00:10:48.710
us to write a query
here, and with just very,

00:10:48.710 --> 00:10:54.110
very simple amount of code, read
it into a pandas data frame.

00:10:54.110 --> 00:10:56.560
So here's that data
frame, or a preview of it.

00:10:56.560 --> 00:10:59.520
The source here is
the target variable.

00:10:59.520 --> 00:11:05.200
And the headline is
here, on the right.

00:11:05.200 --> 00:11:09.520
So let's do a first attempt
to tokenize the Scikit-Learn.

00:11:09.520 --> 00:11:11.362
And we'll see the problem.

00:11:11.362 --> 00:11:12.820
So the problem here
that you see is

00:11:12.820 --> 00:11:14.650
that the English
text is successfully

00:11:14.650 --> 00:11:17.690
split into its
constituent words,

00:11:17.690 --> 00:11:20.020
but the Japanese texts
left completely alone,

00:11:20.020 --> 00:11:22.760
which is because
Scikit-Learn is just naively

00:11:22.760 --> 00:11:26.090
splitting on the spaces.

00:11:26.090 --> 00:11:30.180
So now, let's use Google's
natural language API.

00:11:30.180 --> 00:11:32.520
We create a language
service client

00:11:32.520 --> 00:11:34.780
from the Google client library.

00:11:34.780 --> 00:11:36.540
And for each piece
of Japanese text,

00:11:36.540 --> 00:11:39.354
we pass it to this
tokenize function.

00:11:39.354 --> 00:11:40.770
What the tokenize
function does is

00:11:40.770 --> 00:11:45.150
it calls the Analyze Syntax
method on that client,

00:11:45.150 --> 00:11:48.960
takes the return value from
that and extract the tokens.

00:11:48.960 --> 00:11:50.970
The tokens are joined
together with spaces

00:11:50.970 --> 00:11:56.130
and inserted back into a new
column in the data frame called

00:11:56.130 --> 00:11:58.470
tokenized text.

00:11:58.470 --> 00:12:01.430
And now, if we run this
last cell in the Notebook,

00:12:01.430 --> 00:12:03.999
we'll just show that
we've solved the problem.

00:12:03.999 --> 00:12:05.540
We've run the exact
same set of code.

00:12:05.540 --> 00:12:07.580
But this time, the Japanese
text is successfully

00:12:07.580 --> 00:12:09.640
split into words.

00:12:09.640 --> 00:12:11.640
It's a very simple example.

00:12:11.640 --> 00:12:13.300
But I think what's
cool about it is it

00:12:13.300 --> 00:12:16.970
hides a ton of complexity.

00:12:16.970 --> 00:12:19.550
So here we go.

00:12:22.022 --> 00:12:23.730
Oh, by the way, the
links to all the code

00:12:23.730 --> 00:12:26.063
are going to be available in
the last slide of the deck.

00:12:28.020 --> 00:12:30.530
So Google's high-level
machine learning APIs

00:12:30.530 --> 00:12:32.600
can be useful in
preprocessing data.

00:12:32.600 --> 00:12:35.000
But how about
infrastructure as a service?

00:12:35.000 --> 00:12:37.310
Are we really going to
talk about virtual machines

00:12:37.310 --> 00:12:39.200
in this talk about
machine learning?

00:12:39.200 --> 00:12:41.870
And the answer is
a resounding yes.

00:12:41.870 --> 00:12:44.240
So what kind of
scaling challenges

00:12:44.240 --> 00:12:49.160
do users of Scikit-Learn and
XGBoost typically run into?

00:12:49.160 --> 00:12:51.530
Well, scaling can
require more memory

00:12:51.530 --> 00:12:53.870
if your data set is very large.

00:12:53.870 --> 00:12:58.040
It can require more
CPU or GPU or TPU

00:12:58.040 --> 00:13:00.350
if the best ML
algorithm for the task

00:13:00.350 --> 00:13:02.570
is computationally intense.

00:13:02.570 --> 00:13:04.730
And it can require more
processes or threads

00:13:04.730 --> 00:13:08.760
if you have a big
hyperparameter space to explore.

00:13:08.760 --> 00:13:11.550
And so when you need
to scale, you typically

00:13:11.550 --> 00:13:14.522
think about
parallelizing your work.

00:13:14.522 --> 00:13:15.980
So let me talk
about different ways

00:13:15.980 --> 00:13:17.525
you can parallelize your work.

00:13:17.525 --> 00:13:21.611
Hyperparameter tuning is
embarrassingly parallel--

00:13:21.611 --> 00:13:24.110
embarrassingly parallel because
it's so easy to parallelize.

00:13:24.110 --> 00:13:27.170
The only information that needs
to be shared from the workers

00:13:27.170 --> 00:13:29.780
back up to the scheduler
is just the final results,

00:13:29.780 --> 00:13:32.600
and then the schedule
just picks the best one.

00:13:32.600 --> 00:13:38.360
Both SK-Learn and XGBoost allow
HP tuning to be parallelized.

00:13:38.360 --> 00:13:40.280
Also, there are
ensemble methods of ML,

00:13:40.280 --> 00:13:43.520
like random forests,
that can be parallelized.

00:13:43.520 --> 00:13:47.240
And preprocessing
can be parallelized.

00:13:47.240 --> 00:13:49.900
So you can switch
from pandas to Dask.

00:13:49.900 --> 00:13:52.400
Dask implements the
pandas dataframe API,

00:13:52.400 --> 00:13:54.870
but does so in a
parallelizable way.

00:13:54.870 --> 00:13:56.480
And it's super cool.

00:13:56.480 --> 00:13:59.722
And I think when many people
think about parallelization,

00:13:59.722 --> 00:14:01.430
they immediately think
about distribution

00:14:01.430 --> 00:14:03.860
across multiple nodes.

00:14:03.860 --> 00:14:05.900
And I think that's
because our industry has

00:14:05.900 --> 00:14:09.380
made a ton of amazing progress
in the last three years

00:14:09.380 --> 00:14:11.330
through distributed computing.

00:14:11.330 --> 00:14:13.910
However, often times,
you can actually

00:14:13.910 --> 00:14:16.730
get a lot done
with a single node,

00:14:16.730 --> 00:14:19.880
and it can be really easy to do.

00:14:19.880 --> 00:14:23.330
Using large compute
instances to do your work

00:14:23.330 --> 00:14:27.170
is becoming increasingly
more and more of a reality,

00:14:27.170 --> 00:14:29.930
as costs come down and
the capacity of nodes

00:14:29.930 --> 00:14:31.460
increases dramatically.

00:14:31.460 --> 00:14:33.380
So here to tell you more
about this is David.

00:14:33.380 --> 00:14:36.020
David Cournapeau
leads the ML team

00:14:36.020 --> 00:14:38.900
at Cogent Labs, which is
a business in Japan that

00:14:38.900 --> 00:14:42.920
specializes in applying deep
learning to B2B markets.

00:14:42.920 --> 00:14:46.730
And as I mentioned, in 2007,
he created Scikit-Learn.

00:14:46.730 --> 00:14:48.404
Thanks.

00:14:48.404 --> 00:14:49.570
DAVID COURNAPEAU: Thank you.

00:14:49.570 --> 00:14:50.236
Can you hear me?

00:14:50.236 --> 00:14:54.870
[APPLAUSE]

00:14:54.870 --> 00:14:55.870
It's good for the sound?

00:14:55.870 --> 00:14:57.340
You can all hear me?

00:14:57.340 --> 00:14:57.965
OK.

00:14:57.965 --> 00:14:59.000
So thank you, Steve.

00:14:59.000 --> 00:15:00.000
Thank you for having me.

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:01.170
I'm excited to be here.

00:15:01.170 --> 00:15:03.510
It's the first time
at Google Next.

00:15:03.510 --> 00:15:07.690
So as Steve mentioned, I've
been working for almost one year

00:15:07.690 --> 00:15:08.790
now at Cogent Labs.

00:15:08.790 --> 00:15:13.500
It's a startup in Japan, Tokyo,
where we do deep learning.

00:15:13.500 --> 00:15:15.960
And so we try to apply deep
learning to programs that

00:15:15.960 --> 00:15:17.524
were difficult to sort before.

00:15:17.524 --> 00:15:18.940
So, for example,
we have a product

00:15:18.940 --> 00:15:22.350
called Tegaki, which is Japanese
handwriting recognition using

00:15:22.350 --> 00:15:23.640
deep learning.

00:15:23.640 --> 00:15:26.580
We have a product called Kaidoku
for document understanding.

00:15:26.580 --> 00:15:29.664
And we have also a system to
do time series prediction.

00:15:29.664 --> 00:15:31.080
But today, actually,
I am not here

00:15:31.080 --> 00:15:33.000
to talk about deep
learning, but I'm here

00:15:33.000 --> 00:15:36.226
to talk about a lot of the work
that happened before, like when

00:15:36.226 --> 00:15:37.350
you need to deal with data.

00:15:37.350 --> 00:15:40.417
Because Steve already mentioned,
when you do a machine learning,

00:15:40.417 --> 00:15:42.000
you need to actually
spend quite a bit

00:15:42.000 --> 00:15:43.350
of time on the preprocessing.

00:15:43.350 --> 00:15:47.820
So it's the unsexy part of data
science and machine learning.

00:15:47.820 --> 00:15:49.920
Realistically speaking,
when you work on product,

00:15:49.920 --> 00:15:52.560
that's most of what you
spend your time on, more than

00:15:52.560 --> 00:15:53.967
on the models themselves.

00:15:53.967 --> 00:15:55.800
And this part is actually
not that different

00:15:55.800 --> 00:15:57.720
whether you're using
machine learning,

00:15:57.720 --> 00:16:00.227
like traditional machine
learning like Scikit-Learn,

00:16:00.227 --> 00:16:02.560
or whether you're using deep
learning through frameworks

00:16:02.560 --> 00:16:05.760
like TensorFlow.

00:16:05.760 --> 00:16:09.510
I want to talk a bit more
about that part, actually.

00:16:09.510 --> 00:16:14.165
So the example I will use to
illustrate, a bit, my points,

00:16:14.165 --> 00:16:16.230
is using one of the
project I've been

00:16:16.230 --> 00:16:18.660
working on at Cogent Labs
for the financial time series

00:16:18.660 --> 00:16:20.130
forecasting.

00:16:20.130 --> 00:16:22.410
So we did this project
in coordination

00:16:22.410 --> 00:16:25.740
with one of the biggest
brokerage company in Japan.

00:16:25.740 --> 00:16:27.240
And we use, basically,
deep learning

00:16:27.240 --> 00:16:30.980
to do time series prediction
for real time volume prediction

00:16:30.980 --> 00:16:32.730
on the Tokyo Stock Exchange.

00:16:32.730 --> 00:16:35.420
And the product has been live
since last month, actually.

00:16:38.130 --> 00:16:41.490
But to do the deep learning
algorithm, you need data.

00:16:41.490 --> 00:16:44.310
And it's a truism today--

00:16:44.310 --> 00:16:46.720
the more data you
have, the better it is.

00:16:46.720 --> 00:16:48.330
And so you have
this famous paper

00:16:48.330 --> 00:16:52.020
from like 10 years
ago by Alon Halevy

00:16:52.020 --> 00:16:56.070
and Peter Norvig about the
unreasonable effectiveness

00:16:56.070 --> 00:16:56.730
of data.

00:16:56.730 --> 00:16:59.780
So Peter Norvig is the head of
research at Google, actually.

00:16:59.780 --> 00:17:03.030
Another thing he mentioned
early on when he was at Google

00:17:03.030 --> 00:17:05.730
is, "At Google, we don't
have better algorithm,

00:17:05.730 --> 00:17:06.869
but we have better data.

00:17:06.869 --> 00:17:08.040
We have more data."

00:17:08.040 --> 00:17:10.079
And like he said, some
things are really--

00:17:10.079 --> 00:17:12.310
he used that already
intuitively before,

00:17:12.310 --> 00:17:16.920
but some things that
really stayed with him when

00:17:16.920 --> 00:17:19.920
he was at Google was how
even a simple algorithm

00:17:19.920 --> 00:17:21.920
can work so well when
you have a lot of data.

00:17:21.920 --> 00:17:24.690
So this whole argument about
the unreasonable effectiveness

00:17:24.690 --> 00:17:27.599
of data and how
you can generally

00:17:27.599 --> 00:17:31.320
improve your product,
not by changing the model

00:17:31.320 --> 00:17:33.600
but just by using more data.

00:17:33.600 --> 00:17:36.750
So with this whole thing with,
you know, it's almost a fad,

00:17:36.750 --> 00:17:39.750
at that point, about large data,
big data, and the more data

00:17:39.750 --> 00:17:41.460
you have and the better it is.

00:17:41.460 --> 00:17:45.874
You have more and more data,
internet, blah, blah, blah.

00:17:45.874 --> 00:17:46.540
What can you do?

00:17:46.540 --> 00:17:47.990
Do you need to do
distribute computing?

00:17:47.990 --> 00:17:49.364
Do you need to
always use Hadoop?

00:17:49.364 --> 00:17:51.290
But I only have two engineers.

00:17:51.290 --> 00:17:53.160
How I can do this, right?

00:17:53.160 --> 00:17:57.600
So at that point, it's
almost a fad, but still

00:17:57.600 --> 00:18:00.990
a real thing for some companies,
where you have large data

00:18:00.990 --> 00:18:02.970
to use for your algorithm.

00:18:02.970 --> 00:18:05.735
And the problem is, when you
have a lot of staff engineers,

00:18:05.735 --> 00:18:08.490
they like using tools
for distribute computing,

00:18:08.490 --> 00:18:10.600
because it's fun and
you have a lot to do.

00:18:10.600 --> 00:18:12.430
So you have all these
great technologies

00:18:12.430 --> 00:18:15.180
like Hadoop, Spark, Kafka, Beam.

00:18:15.180 --> 00:18:18.210
Some of them will be mentioned
or have been mentioned already

00:18:18.210 --> 00:18:19.780
in this conference.

00:18:19.780 --> 00:18:22.380
And so as I said, engineers love
working with distribute data

00:18:22.380 --> 00:18:25.390
system because it's fun.

00:18:25.390 --> 00:18:31.114
But is large data really
eating the world, so to speak?

00:18:31.114 --> 00:18:32.280
So this paper I really like.

00:18:32.280 --> 00:18:34.950
It's a bit old now, but I
think the lessons are still

00:18:34.950 --> 00:18:36.960
relevant today,
like "Nobody ever

00:18:36.960 --> 00:18:39.420
got fired for using Hadoop."

00:18:39.420 --> 00:18:42.720
And again, the numbers are a bit
old, because they're from 2012.

00:18:42.720 --> 00:18:46.640
But I've noticed the same thing
in my previous job and even

00:18:46.640 --> 00:18:48.150
currently.

00:18:48.150 --> 00:18:50.640
So in this paper
in 2012, they look

00:18:50.640 --> 00:18:54.510
at what was the median
size of a job in Hadoop?

00:18:54.510 --> 00:18:59.220
Microsoft and Yahoo, the median
size was below 14 gigabytes.

00:18:59.220 --> 00:19:03.660
At Facebook, 90% of the Hadoop
jobs they were using as a time

00:19:03.660 --> 00:19:07.008
were for a job input size
of below 100 gigabyte.

00:19:11.000 --> 00:19:14.050
And so there's this joke where
a lot of people like to say,

00:19:14.050 --> 00:19:15.170
oh, I have a lot of data.

00:19:15.170 --> 00:19:16.390
I have a gigabyte of data.

00:19:16.390 --> 00:19:18.160
So I use Hadoop.

00:19:18.160 --> 00:19:23.920
And so there's this famous
blogger and conference

00:19:23.920 --> 00:19:27.250
speaker, Gary Bernhardt, where
he had this joke, like, I have

00:19:27.250 --> 00:19:29.920
this new consulting
services where you bring me

00:19:29.920 --> 00:19:32.170
your big data problem,
and I will tell you, well,

00:19:32.170 --> 00:19:34.810
your data actually is
small enough to fit in RAM.

00:19:34.810 --> 00:19:36.610
You pay me $10,000,
and then you don't

00:19:36.610 --> 00:19:39.360
have to spend half a million
dollars on infrastructure.

00:19:39.360 --> 00:19:41.800
And if it sounds
almost trivial--

00:19:41.800 --> 00:19:44.140
you're like, I would never
make that mistake, right?

00:19:44.140 --> 00:19:46.210
Of course if you have
a small data set,

00:19:46.210 --> 00:19:47.800
I will not use Hadoop.

00:19:47.800 --> 00:19:49.410
But that's something
I keep saying.

00:19:49.410 --> 00:19:51.970
At the beginning of this
year, PyData, in London,

00:19:51.970 --> 00:19:54.370
I was talking to this guy
working for a hedge fund.

00:19:54.370 --> 00:19:55.990
And he said when
he joined, there

00:19:55.990 --> 00:19:59.080
was this team of 15 people doing
a scalar distributed system

00:19:59.080 --> 00:20:03.144
for a dataset that
was 40 gigabyte.

00:20:03.144 --> 00:20:06.890
He fired all of them
and built a system

00:20:06.890 --> 00:20:11.060
that was a bit better design.

00:20:11.060 --> 00:20:16.081
So that's one big lesson
I want you to remember.

00:20:16.081 --> 00:20:17.830
Maybe some people don't
make that mistake.

00:20:17.830 --> 00:20:19.435
But when you have a
bit less experience,

00:20:19.435 --> 00:20:20.726
it's easier to do this mistake.

00:20:20.726 --> 00:20:23.230
But don't over-architect.

00:20:23.230 --> 00:20:26.710
And the thing is, when
you do machine learning,

00:20:26.710 --> 00:20:30.760
the typical data size for ad-hoc
analyses is often not that big.

00:20:30.760 --> 00:20:32.270
Sure, you have
more and more data.

00:20:32.270 --> 00:20:34.930
And of course, if you're Google,
you have a large data set.

00:20:34.930 --> 00:20:37.120
Other companies have
a very large data set.

00:20:37.120 --> 00:20:42.340
But most companies, they
don't have that big of a data.

00:20:42.340 --> 00:20:44.860
And the thing that is
interesting, as well,

00:20:44.860 --> 00:20:46.750
maybe you're storing
more and more data,

00:20:46.750 --> 00:20:49.480
but the data you need to look at
when you look for a new problem

00:20:49.480 --> 00:20:52.990
to solve, or maybe to improve
an existing algorithm,

00:20:52.990 --> 00:20:55.176
you don't need to use
all the data at once.

00:20:55.176 --> 00:20:57.550
Especially at the beginning,
when you do ad-hoc analyses,

00:20:57.550 --> 00:20:59.660
you're not sure exactly
what you're looking for.

00:20:59.660 --> 00:21:02.140
You want to understand
a bit better your data.

00:21:02.140 --> 00:21:05.710
You can use a subset of this
data to do the analysis.

00:21:05.710 --> 00:21:07.210
So the size of the
data for analysis

00:21:07.210 --> 00:21:11.020
is not the same size as the
data you need for storing.

00:21:11.020 --> 00:21:13.980
It's very easy to
underestimate the engineering

00:21:13.980 --> 00:21:17.932
work you need to deal with
effective data architecture.

00:21:17.932 --> 00:21:19.390
For people who've
used Hadoop, they

00:21:19.390 --> 00:21:22.450
know you don't set up Hadoop in
one hour and then you're done

00:21:22.450 --> 00:21:23.740
and you're using it.

00:21:23.740 --> 00:21:27.860
You need people to take care
of it, like masternodes fails,

00:21:27.860 --> 00:21:30.445
like some of your hardware
fail and you need to take--

00:21:30.445 --> 00:21:33.739
that's a lot of work
to deal with it.

00:21:33.739 --> 00:21:35.530
And especially when
you're a small company,

00:21:35.530 --> 00:21:38.200
I think it's easy to
underestimate the loss of what

00:21:38.200 --> 00:21:39.270
I call data agility.

00:21:39.270 --> 00:21:42.880
That is the ability
to be able to not

00:21:42.880 --> 00:21:46.030
scale the amount of data, to
scale the number of experiments

00:21:46.030 --> 00:21:47.290
you can do with the data.

00:21:47.290 --> 00:21:50.680
Like having data this small,
you can easily visualize it.

00:21:50.680 --> 00:21:52.640
You can understand it.

00:21:52.640 --> 00:21:56.390
It's really important for some
class of problems as well.

00:21:56.390 --> 00:21:59.830
And again, most of the
work on machine learning

00:21:59.830 --> 00:22:02.410
when you work on product
is not on the model itself,

00:22:02.410 --> 00:22:04.130
but everything around.

00:22:04.130 --> 00:22:06.610
So if you optimize
the part where

00:22:06.610 --> 00:22:09.310
you need to have ad-hoc
analysis-- when you need

00:22:09.310 --> 00:22:12.400
to do an ad-hoc analysis,
you should optimize that part

00:22:12.400 --> 00:22:17.170
to be simple, where all your
data scientists can work

00:22:17.170 --> 00:22:20.110
on a set up where they don't
have to fight the system

00:22:20.110 --> 00:22:21.210
to get the data.

00:22:21.210 --> 00:22:22.900
You can get,
actually, a lot back.

00:22:22.900 --> 00:22:25.740
Because you can be faster
and you can develop new model

00:22:25.740 --> 00:22:27.870
faster, understand
your data faster,

00:22:27.870 --> 00:22:29.020
or plot the data faster.

00:22:35.190 --> 00:22:40.860
So what was the link of this
observation with Google Cloud?

00:22:40.860 --> 00:22:44.360
So again, data are
getting bigger.

00:22:44.360 --> 00:22:46.860
I had mentioned before, don't
try to over-architect too much

00:22:46.860 --> 00:22:48.875
your data architecture.

00:22:48.875 --> 00:22:50.250
But something that
is interesting

00:22:50.250 --> 00:22:54.150
when you think about
cloud services,

00:22:54.150 --> 00:22:57.900
and GCP in particular, is,
you can get a really, really

00:22:57.900 --> 00:22:59.760
large VM now.

00:22:59.760 --> 00:23:01.710
Recently, GCP announced
that you can have VM

00:23:01.710 --> 00:23:04.290
with four terabyte of memory.

00:23:04.290 --> 00:23:08.300
That's a lot of data you can
keep in memory on one machine.

00:23:08.300 --> 00:23:11.950
And when you look at the
trend of what is typical data

00:23:11.950 --> 00:23:14.410
size that people
use in data science,

00:23:14.410 --> 00:23:17.320
actually, you see it's
kind of plateauing.

00:23:17.320 --> 00:23:21.460
So it's just one data sample,
but you have the "KD Nugget" is

00:23:21.460 --> 00:23:23.292
a famous data science web site.

00:23:23.292 --> 00:23:24.750
And every day--
every year, sorry--

00:23:24.750 --> 00:23:27.760
they do a survey to ask
people, what is a typical data

00:23:27.760 --> 00:23:29.710
size you're handing, et cetera?

00:23:29.710 --> 00:23:31.810
And actually, you see that
for the last few years,

00:23:31.810 --> 00:23:35.260
the median data size of the
data that people look at

00:23:35.260 --> 00:23:38.547
is plateauing at a
few hundred gigabytes.

00:23:38.547 --> 00:23:40.630
And five years ago, a few
hundred gigabytes, yeah,

00:23:40.630 --> 00:23:43.247
maybe you had to use
Hadoop, or at least you

00:23:43.247 --> 00:23:44.080
need to think a bit.

00:23:44.080 --> 00:23:45.580
You're [INAUDIBLE].

00:23:45.580 --> 00:23:47.680
But today, a few
hundred of gigabytes--

00:23:47.680 --> 00:23:50.302
if I have to deal with this
today, I know what I will do.

00:23:50.302 --> 00:23:51.760
If I can put the
data on the cloud,

00:23:51.760 --> 00:23:54.460
I would just put
them on a large VM

00:23:54.460 --> 00:23:56.560
where I can load
everything in RAM.

00:23:56.560 --> 00:23:58.720
And then everything
becomes much easier for me,

00:23:58.720 --> 00:24:01.160
and I don't have to over think
too much of the engineering

00:24:01.160 --> 00:24:03.824
part.

00:24:03.824 --> 00:24:04.990
It's not just about the RAM.

00:24:04.990 --> 00:24:06.610
OK, you use a CPU.

00:24:06.610 --> 00:24:07.920
It's kind of obvious.

00:24:07.920 --> 00:24:10.960
But also, at least on GCP, you
have, also like fast local IU

00:24:10.960 --> 00:24:12.070
available, right?

00:24:12.070 --> 00:24:14.810
You can't create VMs
that have NVME SSD, where

00:24:14.810 --> 00:24:18.230
you have more than one gigabyte
per second, like throughput,

00:24:18.230 --> 00:24:19.312
when reading from disk.

00:24:19.312 --> 00:24:20.770
So a lot of these
together means we

00:24:20.770 --> 00:24:23.770
can have very powerful
machine where you can actually

00:24:23.770 --> 00:24:27.940
get a lot done without going
too early to distribution.

00:24:27.940 --> 00:24:29.796
The advantages are
fairly obvious.

00:24:29.796 --> 00:24:31.420
If you're a scientist
who are maybe not

00:24:31.420 --> 00:24:33.470
so strong on the software
engineering side,

00:24:33.470 --> 00:24:35.350
they can focus on
their main expertise.

00:24:35.350 --> 00:24:37.920
So they don't need to spend
time learning about Hadoop,

00:24:37.920 --> 00:24:40.050
about Pig, Hive, whatever.

00:24:40.050 --> 00:24:43.837
And they keep all the
interactivity of the tools

00:24:43.837 --> 00:24:46.420
that people like to use in data
science, especially in Python,

00:24:46.420 --> 00:24:49.450
and some of the tools that
Steve mentioned before.

00:24:49.450 --> 00:24:51.970
So you keep the interactivity
on a single machine.

00:24:51.970 --> 00:24:55.840
And you keep-- using Notebook
is easy, using [INAUDIBLE]..

00:24:55.840 --> 00:25:00.710
All of that stays easy.

00:25:00.710 --> 00:25:02.920
So concretely, if you look at--

00:25:02.920 --> 00:25:05.940
I was mentioning before
this project we did

00:25:05.940 --> 00:25:08.770
with investment bank in Japan.

00:25:08.770 --> 00:25:10.860
So we have a fairly
typical problem.

00:25:10.860 --> 00:25:12.570
We use deep learning
after, but it

00:25:12.570 --> 00:25:15.400
doesn't matter so much for
what I'm talking about here.

00:25:15.400 --> 00:25:16.835
So we use market data.

00:25:16.835 --> 00:25:17.710
So we have tick data.

00:25:17.710 --> 00:25:19.320
So tick data, for
people not familiar,

00:25:19.320 --> 00:25:22.260
is just the price of each
stocks at each moment

00:25:22.260 --> 00:25:24.030
it's coming from the market.

00:25:24.030 --> 00:25:27.770
So concretely, we have between
1 and 200 gigabyte of CSV files.

00:25:27.770 --> 00:25:30.120
It's already semi-aggregated.

00:25:30.120 --> 00:25:32.790
We have 50 million
points per month.

00:25:32.790 --> 00:25:34.290
So we had a bit
more than two years,

00:25:34.290 --> 00:25:37.670
so we have a more than
one billion data points.

00:25:37.670 --> 00:25:39.900
And some of the
analysis we had to do

00:25:39.900 --> 00:25:43.515
was pretty easy to
parallelize if you want,

00:25:43.515 --> 00:25:44.950
like some preprocessing.

00:25:44.950 --> 00:25:48.100
It's each row independently,
so you could parallelize that

00:25:48.100 --> 00:25:48.690
fairly easily.

00:25:48.690 --> 00:25:51.584
Some things were a bit
less easy to parallelize,

00:25:51.584 --> 00:25:52.250
like resampling.

00:25:52.250 --> 00:25:54.880
You had this thing called
open bin detection.

00:25:54.880 --> 00:25:57.030
It's boring, but basically,
it's kind of retrosave.

00:25:57.030 --> 00:25:59.432
So it's more difficult
to parallelize.

00:25:59.432 --> 00:26:01.140
And at the beginning
of the preprocessing

00:26:01.140 --> 00:26:02.735
we had was quite
resource hungry.

00:26:02.735 --> 00:26:04.110
You needed more
than 100 gigabyte

00:26:04.110 --> 00:26:08.376
of RAM just to deal with
one of the CSV files we had.

00:26:08.376 --> 00:26:09.850
So to make it a
bit more concrete,

00:26:09.850 --> 00:26:12.335
I just show a bit
how it looks like.

00:26:12.335 --> 00:26:14.460
So you don't need to really
understand the numbers.

00:26:14.460 --> 00:26:17.440
But when you use market
data, the problem

00:26:17.440 --> 00:26:22.250
is, each market data provider
has different conventions.

00:26:22.250 --> 00:26:24.514
So you need to
convert between them.

00:26:24.514 --> 00:26:26.930
You need to convert between
different market data provider

00:26:26.930 --> 00:26:28.250
to a common format.

00:26:28.250 --> 00:26:30.890
So boring, but again,
fairly typical things,

00:26:30.890 --> 00:26:33.830
like removing some characters
in the symbol and all

00:26:33.830 --> 00:26:35.160
of these kind of things.

00:26:35.160 --> 00:26:38.670
And we needed to do that for
quite a few data points--

00:26:38.670 --> 00:26:41.276
several billions.

00:26:41.276 --> 00:26:43.377
And so we need to speed this up.

00:26:43.377 --> 00:26:45.710
And so you may wonder, why
do you need to speed this up?

00:26:45.710 --> 00:26:47.640
Preprocessing, you only
need to do it once.

00:26:47.640 --> 00:26:50.580
You can save that
into a dataframe,

00:26:50.580 --> 00:26:53.250
or maybe you can save that
to a parquet file, which

00:26:53.250 --> 00:26:56.340
is a more modern
[? stylization ?] format that

00:26:56.340 --> 00:26:59.719
can be styled quickly from-- and
to disk from pandas dataframe.

00:26:59.719 --> 00:27:01.260
But the problem is,
at the beginning,

00:27:01.260 --> 00:27:04.350
we were not sure exactly
which kind of preprocessing we

00:27:04.350 --> 00:27:07.080
had to do, because again,
these market data are pretty

00:27:07.080 --> 00:27:08.940
complicated, and
different banks actually

00:27:08.940 --> 00:27:11.460
deal with them differently and
have different conventions.

00:27:11.460 --> 00:27:13.918
So we had to go back and forth
with just the customer quite

00:27:13.918 --> 00:27:15.190
a few times.

00:27:15.190 --> 00:27:18.090
And so the speed of processing
was actually kind of important.

00:27:18.090 --> 00:27:21.125
Because if it takes one
day versus one week,

00:27:21.125 --> 00:27:22.500
it can make a
difference when you

00:27:22.500 --> 00:27:25.420
need to talk to the
customer back and forth.

00:27:25.420 --> 00:27:29.850
And so just by using
larger VM on Google Cloud--

00:27:29.850 --> 00:27:32.910
nothing fancy-- pandas,
your bash scripting,

00:27:32.910 --> 00:27:34.964
a bit of a [? parallel ?]
option to xargs--

00:27:34.964 --> 00:27:36.630
you actually could
get quite a lot done.

00:27:36.630 --> 00:27:40.440
And having the big VM allowed us
to iterate quickly, especially

00:27:40.440 --> 00:27:42.680
in exploratory phases.

00:27:42.680 --> 00:27:44.590
And I think big VMs
really allowed us also

00:27:44.590 --> 00:27:47.610
to convert the CSV very
quickly to some other file

00:27:47.610 --> 00:27:53.040
format that are more efficient
on the disk and the memory.

00:27:53.040 --> 00:27:56.850
So really, the
takeaway here is there

00:27:56.850 --> 00:27:59.315
was this fashion of
always scale horizontally,

00:27:59.315 --> 00:28:01.841
because scaling
up doesn't scale.

00:28:01.841 --> 00:28:03.340
Well, actually, for
a lot of things,

00:28:03.340 --> 00:28:05.070
scaling up actually does scale.

00:28:05.070 --> 00:28:07.470
Especially with the cloud
now, it's quite easy

00:28:07.470 --> 00:28:09.390
to get really large VM.

00:28:09.390 --> 00:28:12.710
And as long as you do
that for ad-hoc analysis,

00:28:12.710 --> 00:28:14.640
I actually think it
works pretty well.

00:28:14.640 --> 00:28:18.540
You can keep iteration quickly
for doing ad-hoc analysis.

00:28:18.540 --> 00:28:21.195
I think VM actually are
getting bigger more quickly

00:28:21.195 --> 00:28:23.200
than data are getting bigger.

00:28:23.200 --> 00:28:24.870
At the same time,
you have two that

00:28:24.870 --> 00:28:26.400
become more memory efficient.

00:28:26.400 --> 00:28:28.560
So first of all, for
pandas, the rule of thumb

00:28:28.560 --> 00:28:30.410
for Wes McKinney, the
creator of pandas,

00:28:30.410 --> 00:28:33.030
is if you need to deal
with a data set of size

00:28:33.030 --> 00:28:36.750
x, generally the rule of thumb
is you need 5x to 10x of RAM

00:28:36.750 --> 00:28:39.900
to deal with this
comfortably into pandas.

00:28:39.900 --> 00:28:44.125
But now, with tools like Arrow,
this factor becomes smaller.

00:28:44.125 --> 00:28:45.750
So it's actually more
memory efficient.

00:28:45.750 --> 00:28:48.600
So for a given amount of RAM,
you actually can get more done

00:28:48.600 --> 00:28:50.700
and get larger data.

00:28:50.700 --> 00:28:52.740
And some of those
lessons, they are not

00:28:52.740 --> 00:28:56.714
specific to just preprocessing
or ad-hoc analysis,

00:28:56.714 --> 00:28:59.130
but actually, you can apply
this in later phases, as well,

00:28:59.130 --> 00:29:00.610
of your project.

00:29:00.610 --> 00:29:03.180
So one of the way I like using
these kind of technologies

00:29:03.180 --> 00:29:05.807
is for testing and
continuous integration.

00:29:05.807 --> 00:29:08.140
Doing continuous integration
for machine learning models

00:29:08.140 --> 00:29:11.264
is pretty difficult. How
do you write unique tests

00:29:11.264 --> 00:29:12.430
for machine learning models?

00:29:12.430 --> 00:29:13.180
How do you write--

00:29:13.180 --> 00:29:16.650
you know, It's not as mature
as for standard software

00:29:16.650 --> 00:29:17.640
engineering.

00:29:17.640 --> 00:29:20.970
And sometimes one of the issues
is that it takes a lot of time

00:29:20.970 --> 00:29:23.650
to just test even simple
things in your models,

00:29:23.650 --> 00:29:25.230
especially when you
do deep learning.

00:29:25.230 --> 00:29:28.110
Well, here, again, if you
can launch on demand, just

00:29:28.110 --> 00:29:31.830
for half an hour, very large
VMs with a lot of cores,

00:29:31.830 --> 00:29:36.040
you can actually get away
with simple solution,

00:29:36.040 --> 00:29:40.350
and you don't need to, again,
over-architect too early.

00:29:40.350 --> 00:29:44.120
All the lessons I'm giving
here, those are simple tricks,

00:29:44.120 --> 00:29:46.487
and maybe it's trivial
for most of you.

00:29:46.487 --> 00:29:48.570
But you can use them not
just for ad-hoc analysis,

00:29:48.570 --> 00:29:54.192
but also for
testing, integration.

00:29:54.192 --> 00:29:57.869
I will give back to Steve.

00:29:57.869 --> 00:29:58.910
STEVE GREENBERG: Perfect.

00:29:58.910 --> 00:29:59.720
Thanks so much.

00:29:59.720 --> 00:30:01.190
You can take that.

00:30:01.190 --> 00:30:02.212
Thanks, David.

00:30:02.212 --> 00:30:06.931
[APPLAUSE]

00:30:06.931 --> 00:30:07.430
Perfect.

00:30:07.430 --> 00:30:11.750
So as you think about
scaling up your training

00:30:11.750 --> 00:30:13.760
on single nodes
on Compute Engine,

00:30:13.760 --> 00:30:15.770
I want to call your
attention to two resources

00:30:15.770 --> 00:30:18.730
that I think can be really
helpful from Google.

00:30:18.730 --> 00:30:22.370
So the first is called
Compute Engine burst training.

00:30:22.370 --> 00:30:23.510
And there's a link there.

00:30:23.510 --> 00:30:25.160
It will also be at
the end of the deck.

00:30:25.160 --> 00:30:26.420
It's all on GitHub.

00:30:26.420 --> 00:30:28.730
It's just a pattern
for doing your work.

00:30:28.730 --> 00:30:30.350
And basically, it's
a set of scripts

00:30:30.350 --> 00:30:34.700
that allow you to automatically
provision a machine,

00:30:34.700 --> 00:30:37.850
kick off training, export
the results of training

00:30:37.850 --> 00:30:40.609
to cloud storage, and
shut down the machine.

00:30:40.609 --> 00:30:42.650
That's all done for you
so that you can basically

00:30:42.650 --> 00:30:45.980
get your code correct
on a sample of your data

00:30:45.980 --> 00:30:47.960
on your local machine,
and then, when

00:30:47.960 --> 00:30:50.480
you want to train on
your full dataset,

00:30:50.480 --> 00:30:52.690
you do that on the
cloud, but you do it

00:30:52.690 --> 00:30:54.900
in a way that
minimizes your costs.

00:30:54.900 --> 00:30:58.040
So you're able to just provision
that four terabyte machine

00:30:58.040 --> 00:31:00.770
for just the period
that you need.

00:31:00.770 --> 00:31:04.170
And the other became available
just about two weeks ago

00:31:04.170 --> 00:31:06.110
on Cloud Launcher.

00:31:06.110 --> 00:31:08.240
It's called the Deep
Learning VM Image.

00:31:08.240 --> 00:31:10.250
And while it says
deep learning, I

00:31:10.250 --> 00:31:14.430
think it's really helpful for
XGBoost and SK-Learn, as well.

00:31:14.430 --> 00:31:17.300
It automatically
installs pandas and NumPy

00:31:17.300 --> 00:31:19.040
and other common dependencies.

00:31:19.040 --> 00:31:21.080
It automatically
starts up Jupyter,

00:31:21.080 --> 00:31:24.140
previsions an SSH tunnel, and
gives you a little command

00:31:24.140 --> 00:31:27.620
to basically browse to Jupyter
from your local machine.

00:31:27.620 --> 00:31:29.330
Again, none of this
is rocket science,

00:31:29.330 --> 00:31:33.680
but it can just make your
life a little bit easier.

00:31:33.680 --> 00:31:37.610
So now, it's time for our
last section, Cloud ML Engine.

00:31:37.610 --> 00:31:40.280
This is all about
productionization

00:31:40.280 --> 00:31:43.280
and taking your code and moving
it into a production service--

00:31:43.280 --> 00:31:45.260
which means that it's
highly available,

00:31:45.260 --> 00:31:47.450
that you have a repeatable
process for deploying

00:31:47.450 --> 00:31:49.310
and updating, and
that it responds

00:31:49.310 --> 00:31:53.450
to real-time changes in demand.

00:31:53.450 --> 00:31:57.800
And so if your service relies
on prediction for real customer

00:31:57.800 --> 00:32:01.700
experiences or if you regularly
retrain your model on new data

00:32:01.700 --> 00:32:06.310
as it comes in, Cloud ML
Engine can be a real help.

00:32:06.310 --> 00:32:08.990
And at this conference,
we have announced

00:32:08.990 --> 00:32:11.120
that Scikit-Learn
and XGBoost are both

00:32:11.120 --> 00:32:13.700
supported for training
and prediction,

00:32:13.700 --> 00:32:17.090
and both services are
generally available.

00:32:17.090 --> 00:32:19.700
So first, let's talk
about prediction.

00:32:19.700 --> 00:32:20.330
It's simple.

00:32:20.330 --> 00:32:23.090
From XGBoost or Scikit-Learn,
just export the model

00:32:23.090 --> 00:32:26.270
to a file, upload it
to Cloud ML Engine.

00:32:26.270 --> 00:32:27.770
You'll have a REST
endpoint that you

00:32:27.770 --> 00:32:30.744
can use to handle predictions.

00:32:30.744 --> 00:32:32.660
Cloud ML Engine runs on
Google infrastructure,

00:32:32.660 --> 00:32:36.152
so Google SREs are
holding the pager.

00:32:36.152 --> 00:32:38.360
And as you'll see in this
demo I'm about to show you,

00:32:38.360 --> 00:32:41.270
it can help you avoid
training-serving skew.

00:32:41.270 --> 00:32:43.490
So training-serving
skew happens when

00:32:43.490 --> 00:32:46.680
you process data differently
in training and in prediction.

00:32:46.680 --> 00:32:49.790
And it can lead your
predictions to just not live up

00:32:49.790 --> 00:32:53.030
to the expectations
set during training.

00:32:53.030 --> 00:32:56.600
And the solution to avoid
training-serving skew,

00:32:56.600 --> 00:32:59.240
or one aspect of avoiding
it, is to make sure

00:32:59.240 --> 00:33:00.800
that you share
your preprocessing

00:33:00.800 --> 00:33:04.070
code between training
and prediction.

00:33:04.070 --> 00:33:09.080
And Cloud ML Engine, together
with Scikit-Learn pipelines,

00:33:09.080 --> 00:33:11.100
can help you do exactly that.

00:33:11.100 --> 00:33:12.740
So let's take a
look at it a demo.

00:33:15.840 --> 00:33:18.030
So again, this demo is
in a Jupyter Notebook.

00:33:18.030 --> 00:33:21.630
And this time, we're going to
predict the weight of babies.

00:33:21.630 --> 00:33:25.014
Again, it's a data set
on BigQuery public data.

00:33:25.014 --> 00:33:27.180
Predict the weight of babies
given basic information

00:33:27.180 --> 00:33:29.638
that we might have available
when an expectant mother walks

00:33:29.638 --> 00:33:30.640
into a clinic.

00:33:30.640 --> 00:33:34.170
We know the age of the
mother and the father,

00:33:34.170 --> 00:33:38.520
the gender of the baby, and
the length of the pregnancy.

00:33:38.520 --> 00:33:41.340
In this demo, we've got a couple
of interesting dependencies

00:33:41.340 --> 00:33:43.840
from Scikit-Learn that
I just want to show you.

00:33:43.840 --> 00:33:46.410
The first is a
dictionary vectorizer.

00:33:46.410 --> 00:33:48.550
This what we use
to one-hot encode.

00:33:48.550 --> 00:33:50.730
So it's a really slick
piece of Scikit-Learn

00:33:50.730 --> 00:33:53.040
that just takes categorical
data and automatically

00:33:53.040 --> 00:33:54.630
one-hot encodes it.

00:33:54.630 --> 00:33:57.720
Some of you may call that dummy
encoding, not one-hot encoding.

00:33:57.720 --> 00:33:59.740
But it basically turns
categoricals into numbers

00:33:59.740 --> 00:34:02.730
so that they're easily
understandable by an ML

00:34:02.730 --> 00:34:04.399
algorithm.

00:34:04.399 --> 00:34:05.940
We're training using
a random forest,

00:34:05.940 --> 00:34:07.740
but it could be any model.

00:34:07.740 --> 00:34:10.110
We're joining together
those two pieces together

00:34:10.110 --> 00:34:12.525
in the Scikit-Learn pipeline.

00:34:12.525 --> 00:34:15.150
We've got utility functions for
splitting our training and test

00:34:15.150 --> 00:34:19.050
set, and joblib for
exporting the model.

00:34:19.050 --> 00:34:22.830
We're going to use the discovery
API from the Google API client

00:34:22.830 --> 00:34:28.110
to issue predictions-- basically
just build up a REST request.

00:34:28.110 --> 00:34:31.570
And we are going to also use
BigQuery to read the data.

00:34:31.570 --> 00:34:32.745
So let's run this.

00:34:35.639 --> 00:34:37.260
Great.

00:34:37.260 --> 00:34:40.780
Again, we load our
data from BigQuery.

00:34:40.780 --> 00:34:43.540
And here's the data.

00:34:43.540 --> 00:34:45.280
The weight here is
the leftmost column.

00:34:45.280 --> 00:34:46.449
That's the target variable.

00:34:46.449 --> 00:34:48.219
The rest of the columns
are the features

00:34:48.219 --> 00:34:50.469
we're using to predict it.

00:34:50.469 --> 00:34:53.949
So let's do some basic data
processing, separating out

00:34:53.949 --> 00:34:57.790
our labels from our
features, making sure

00:34:57.790 --> 00:35:00.160
that our categorical
data are of type string

00:35:00.160 --> 00:35:02.680
so that the dictionary
vectorizer will correctly

00:35:02.680 --> 00:35:04.330
handle them.

00:35:04.330 --> 00:35:08.140
And then splitting our
training and test set

00:35:08.140 --> 00:35:12.004
and constructing the pipeline.

00:35:12.004 --> 00:35:13.670
At this point, we
have a pipeline ready,

00:35:13.670 --> 00:35:16.190
but we actually haven't
done anything with it yet.

00:35:16.190 --> 00:35:20.020
This next step-- again, this
is calling the .fit method--

00:35:20.020 --> 00:35:23.180
fits the pipeline to the data.

00:35:23.180 --> 00:35:26.687
And this output shows us that
the pipeline is correctly fit.

00:35:26.687 --> 00:35:29.270
So what that did is, it actually
ran both steps in succession.

00:35:29.270 --> 00:35:31.460
First, it ran the
dictionary vectorizer.

00:35:31.460 --> 00:35:33.330
Then it trained the model.

00:35:33.330 --> 00:35:35.750
And then on the local machine,
we can then call .predict

00:35:35.750 --> 00:35:38.925
on the pipeline and
get our predictions.

00:35:38.925 --> 00:35:40.550
Now, all of this has
been done locally,

00:35:40.550 --> 00:35:42.290
which is all well
and good until you

00:35:42.290 --> 00:35:44.720
want to run it as part
of a production service.

00:35:44.720 --> 00:35:48.004
So now let's do that.

00:35:48.004 --> 00:35:49.670
So we're going to use
joblib to dump out

00:35:49.670 --> 00:35:52.010
the data to the
local file system

00:35:52.010 --> 00:35:53.570
and then copy it up
to cloud storage.

00:35:56.140 --> 00:35:57.710
So another cool
thing about Notebooks

00:35:57.710 --> 00:35:59.126
here is that we're
mixing together

00:35:59.126 --> 00:36:01.310
a magic that sets an
environment variable

00:36:01.310 --> 00:36:04.500
with a call to the
shell, which calls

00:36:04.500 --> 00:36:06.690
GSU to lend copies of the data.

00:36:06.690 --> 00:36:07.850
So the data's up there now.

00:36:07.850 --> 00:36:11.420
And now, let's create a model.

00:36:11.420 --> 00:36:15.370
You can see I've practiced
this demo nine times.

00:36:15.370 --> 00:36:17.440
All right, we're going
to create the model.

00:36:17.440 --> 00:36:19.450
And the model is just
a container for us

00:36:19.450 --> 00:36:23.350
to then put versions of the
model, which will actually

00:36:23.350 --> 00:36:24.770
serve predictions.

00:36:24.770 --> 00:36:27.350
So the meat of the
work is this command.

00:36:27.350 --> 00:36:29.690
And I'm going to run it
and talk you through it.

00:36:29.690 --> 00:36:31.360
It's a call to GCloud.

00:36:31.360 --> 00:36:35.110
And we're creating a version
of the model asynchronously.

00:36:35.110 --> 00:36:36.910
That just means the
Notebook returns

00:36:36.910 --> 00:36:38.920
and continues
execution while waiting

00:36:38.920 --> 00:36:41.191
for the model to finish.

00:36:41.191 --> 00:36:43.690
We're passing it the model and
version name we just created.

00:36:43.690 --> 00:36:46.570
We're specifying that the
framework is Scikit-Learn.

00:36:46.570 --> 00:36:48.040
So the other two
framework options

00:36:48.040 --> 00:36:51.480
are TensorFlow and XGBoost.

00:36:51.480 --> 00:36:56.550
The runtime version of 1.8 is a
Cloud ML Engine runtime version

00:36:56.550 --> 00:36:58.830
that specifies a
version of Scikit-Learn

00:36:58.830 --> 00:37:00.630
and a version of
other dependencies

00:37:00.630 --> 00:37:03.100
that are available to us.

00:37:03.100 --> 00:37:07.620
We point to that file that we
just uploaded to cloud storage.

00:37:07.620 --> 00:37:10.870
And we also specify that
at all points in time,

00:37:10.870 --> 00:37:13.639
there should at least be
one node servicing traffic.

00:37:13.639 --> 00:37:15.180
I may have mentioned
earlier, but one

00:37:15.180 --> 00:37:17.130
of the cool things
about Cloud ML Engine

00:37:17.130 --> 00:37:19.095
is it scales up
or scales to zero.

00:37:19.095 --> 00:37:20.970
And if you have zero
nodes servicing traffic,

00:37:20.970 --> 00:37:23.050
you pay nothing,
which is really nice.

00:37:23.050 --> 00:37:25.470
But your business may
require you to always

00:37:25.470 --> 00:37:27.450
have one node servicing
data, because there

00:37:27.450 --> 00:37:30.090
is a small latency
cost of tens of seconds

00:37:30.090 --> 00:37:32.600
if you're the first request
to provision a model.

00:37:32.600 --> 00:37:36.120
If you want to avoid
that, you can do this.

00:37:36.120 --> 00:37:40.610
Now let us see if
the model is ready.

00:37:40.610 --> 00:37:41.627
It is not ready yet.

00:37:41.627 --> 00:37:43.460
Let's give it one more
second, and then I'll

00:37:43.460 --> 00:37:45.080
call the one before that.

00:37:45.080 --> 00:37:45.930
All right.

00:37:45.930 --> 00:37:47.012
It's ready.

00:37:47.012 --> 00:37:48.470
So that shows you
about how long it

00:37:48.470 --> 00:37:51.500
takes for the model to deploy.

00:37:51.500 --> 00:37:54.424
And this last cell in the
Notebook is Python code.

00:37:54.424 --> 00:37:55.840
It could be any
language you want.

00:37:55.840 --> 00:37:59.630
This is just an HTTP
request to arrest endpoint.

00:37:59.630 --> 00:38:02.330
We use the discovery API
to make it easy to build up

00:38:02.330 --> 00:38:07.335
the request, and then we issue
it, passing the test instances,

00:38:07.335 --> 00:38:09.680
in the payload.

00:38:09.680 --> 00:38:12.080
And we print the predictions
out to the Notebook.

00:38:12.080 --> 00:38:13.930
And if these numbers
look familiar--

00:38:13.930 --> 00:38:17.450
6.44, [? 72.21, ?]
it's because those

00:38:17.450 --> 00:38:19.162
are the same numbers
that we saw up here,

00:38:19.162 --> 00:38:21.620
which is just an indication
that shows that we've basically

00:38:21.620 --> 00:38:24.082
taken the same logic
that was running locally,

00:38:24.082 --> 00:38:25.540
and we've moved it
up to the cloud.

00:38:31.770 --> 00:38:33.510
But prediction is
usually only part

00:38:33.510 --> 00:38:37.080
of a larger, end-to-end pipeline
that can involve retraining,

00:38:37.080 --> 00:38:38.080
as well.

00:38:38.080 --> 00:38:40.440
So here to tell you a little
bit about how that can work

00:38:40.440 --> 00:38:42.180
is Laurent Candillier.

00:38:42.180 --> 00:38:44.635
Laurent is a senior
data scientist at Ocado.

00:38:44.635 --> 00:38:46.176
LAURENT CANDILLIER:
Thank you, Steve.

00:38:46.176 --> 00:38:47.213
[APPLAUSE]

00:38:47.213 --> 00:38:48.546
STEVE GREENBERG: You're welcome.

00:38:51.269 --> 00:38:52.810
LAURENT CANDILLIER:
Thank you, Steve.

00:38:52.810 --> 00:38:55.622
Let me start with
a quick context.

00:38:55.622 --> 00:38:58.690
Ocado has been an online
retailer in the UK

00:38:58.690 --> 00:39:00.460
since 15 years.

00:39:00.460 --> 00:39:03.640
And it has known great success.

00:39:03.640 --> 00:39:07.330
Thousands of order are placed
every day through our website

00:39:07.330 --> 00:39:09.100
or application.

00:39:09.100 --> 00:39:11.710
Then products are
picked up by hundreds

00:39:11.710 --> 00:39:15.820
of robots in our huge,
automated warehouses,

00:39:15.820 --> 00:39:17.650
and happy drivers
come to your homes

00:39:17.650 --> 00:39:21.270
to deliver your goods
whenever you want.

00:39:21.270 --> 00:39:23.520
And with time, Ocado
has also become

00:39:23.520 --> 00:39:24.910
more than just a retailer.

00:39:24.910 --> 00:39:27.360
It has become a tech
company robust enough

00:39:27.360 --> 00:39:29.640
to sell its
technology to others,

00:39:29.640 --> 00:39:33.180
from the website and the app
to the full system of delivery

00:39:33.180 --> 00:39:35.900
using the full automated
warehouses and more

00:39:35.900 --> 00:39:37.680
big data and AI.

00:39:37.680 --> 00:39:40.470
Some of the biggest
retailers in the world

00:39:40.470 --> 00:39:43.260
join our adventure,
like Morrisons in UK;

00:39:43.260 --> 00:39:46.500
[INAUDIBLE],, in Spain; Casino
in France; Sobeys in Canada;

00:39:46.500 --> 00:39:51.141
ICA in Sweden; and Kroger
here in United States.

00:39:51.141 --> 00:39:52.890
So I have five minutes
to go to the point.

00:39:52.890 --> 00:39:55.590
So let me jump to
an example where

00:39:55.590 --> 00:39:58.220
the experience of Ocado
in big data and AI

00:39:58.220 --> 00:40:00.160
can help other retailer.

00:40:00.160 --> 00:40:02.700
Fraud detection is
a classical problem

00:40:02.700 --> 00:40:05.340
that we face in online retail--

00:40:05.340 --> 00:40:08.040
orders being delivered
and never paid for.

00:40:08.040 --> 00:40:09.750
How can we face that?

00:40:09.750 --> 00:40:12.030
With machine
learning, for example.

00:40:12.030 --> 00:40:14.330
Features can be
derived from orders

00:40:14.330 --> 00:40:17.850
and passed, along with the
non-class of failed payments

00:40:17.850 --> 00:40:20.190
or not, to a machine
learning algorithm

00:40:20.190 --> 00:40:22.380
that will learn a
model that will then

00:40:22.380 --> 00:40:25.845
be able to predict if a new
incoming order will probably

00:40:25.845 --> 00:40:29.730
be associated with
failed payment or not.

00:40:29.730 --> 00:40:32.900
Type of frauds that
such ML models can learn

00:40:32.900 --> 00:40:37.620
is if you pass more than three
orders in the future for more

00:40:37.620 --> 00:40:42.000
than 500 pounds each and you
let us less than 24 hours

00:40:42.000 --> 00:40:47.320
to do the delivery, well,
your orders are suspicious.

00:40:47.320 --> 00:40:48.870
And remember one
important thing.

00:40:48.870 --> 00:40:50.630
We are speaking
about big data here,

00:40:50.630 --> 00:40:52.420
with all the retailers
in the world that

00:40:52.420 --> 00:40:53.970
joined our adventure.

00:40:53.970 --> 00:40:56.190
So the big question
here is how to embrace

00:40:56.190 --> 00:40:58.410
the challenge of
quickly building

00:40:58.410 --> 00:41:03.620
scalable machine learning
solutions ready for production.

00:41:03.620 --> 00:41:05.600
As quickly as I
jumped to the problem,

00:41:05.600 --> 00:41:08.150
let me jump to the solution.

00:41:08.150 --> 00:41:10.160
It's called Google
Cloud Platform--

00:41:10.160 --> 00:41:14.710
a great environment to run
machine learning on the clouds.

00:41:14.710 --> 00:41:18.850
You can start with your data
lying in Google BigQuery,

00:41:18.850 --> 00:41:22.330
and run some standard SQL
to join your data coming

00:41:22.330 --> 00:41:28.960
from different resources, and
run some initial computation

00:41:28.960 --> 00:41:30.810
of features.

00:41:30.810 --> 00:41:33.100
You can then go
to Google Dataflow

00:41:33.100 --> 00:41:37.710
to run some Apache Beam to
run some more complex data

00:41:37.710 --> 00:41:39.630
processing like
data normalization,

00:41:39.630 --> 00:41:44.070
for example, or data
transfer to other resources,

00:41:44.070 --> 00:41:50.190
like Datastore, that can be used
as a first data access solution

00:41:50.190 --> 00:41:53.490
to expose some of the
precomputed feature you will

00:41:53.490 --> 00:41:55.930
need at serving time.

00:41:55.930 --> 00:41:59.310
You can also use Google Cloud
Storage to store all your data

00:41:59.310 --> 00:42:04.980
sets as files that will then be
read by Google Cloud ML Engine

00:42:04.980 --> 00:42:09.730
to learn a model and expose
it as a REST endpoints

00:42:09.730 --> 00:42:15.550
and thus be ready to serve
a prediction in real time.

00:42:15.550 --> 00:42:17.370
And here is the important thing.

00:42:17.370 --> 00:42:21.570
When we run the analysis of
comparing machine learning

00:42:21.570 --> 00:42:24.580
algorithms to face
this problem, we've

00:42:24.580 --> 00:42:28.200
found that XGBoost,
in many cases,

00:42:28.200 --> 00:42:33.950
were the algorithm reaching the
best predictive performances.

00:42:33.950 --> 00:42:37.150
But we chose to go to TensorFlow
neural network instead,

00:42:37.150 --> 00:42:41.280
because it was embedded
in Google Cloud ML Engine,

00:42:41.280 --> 00:42:47.860
and thus, we were allowed to
train and predict in the cloud.

00:42:47.860 --> 00:42:50.830
And thus, we were
offering a solution

00:42:50.830 --> 00:42:55.280
to quickly go to prediction
and to be scalable.

00:42:55.280 --> 00:42:58.540
We would also have liked to use,
for example, decision trees,

00:42:58.540 --> 00:43:00.460
for example, in
Scikit-Learn, to be

00:43:00.460 --> 00:43:04.420
able to understand the
outputs of the models

00:43:04.420 --> 00:43:05.620
and the prediction.

00:43:05.620 --> 00:43:10.030
But again, we chose to go to
TensorFlow neural network.

00:43:10.030 --> 00:43:12.940
And basically, with the
work of Steve's team,

00:43:12.940 --> 00:43:15.680
this conscious decision
is not needed anymore.

00:43:15.680 --> 00:43:18.910
We can take the best of all
machine learning algorithm

00:43:18.910 --> 00:43:23.290
using XGBoost and
Scikit-Learn on the cloud,

00:43:23.290 --> 00:43:27.590
deploy easily, train,
and predict on the cloud.

00:43:27.590 --> 00:43:30.735
And so we can now have
better predictive performance

00:43:30.735 --> 00:43:32.890
with XGBoost.

00:43:32.890 --> 00:43:35.230
And the explanation
for fraud detection

00:43:35.230 --> 00:43:38.140
can be important, because
it can be interesting,

00:43:38.140 --> 00:43:40.660
if you provide the
information to a human,

00:43:40.660 --> 00:43:46.210
to explain that you think
that this order is fraudulent

00:43:46.210 --> 00:43:48.850
because of the basket
content or because

00:43:48.850 --> 00:43:50.350
of the past behavior
of the customer

00:43:50.350 --> 00:43:53.970
or because of the payment
method that is used.

00:43:53.970 --> 00:43:57.830
But then if you want to really
take decision automatically,

00:43:57.830 --> 00:43:59.660
for legal reasons,
you need to be

00:43:59.660 --> 00:44:01.140
able to explain
your predictions.

00:44:01.140 --> 00:44:04.800
So this explainability,
for us, is important.

00:44:04.800 --> 00:44:07.370
So basically, big
data, machine learning

00:44:07.370 --> 00:44:08.810
made easy on the cloud.

00:44:08.810 --> 00:44:09.980
What's next, Steve?

00:44:09.980 --> 00:44:10.980
STEVE GREENBERG: Great.

00:44:10.980 --> 00:44:11.980
Thanks, Laurent.

00:44:11.980 --> 00:44:18.480
[APPLAUSE]

00:44:18.480 --> 00:44:19.290
Awesome.

00:44:19.290 --> 00:44:20.740
I'm always happy to
take credit for things,

00:44:20.740 --> 00:44:22.620
but I also want to just note
that I saw [? Marielle ?]

00:44:22.620 --> 00:44:25.080
and [? Robbie ?] walk into
the room a few minutes ago.

00:44:25.080 --> 00:44:26.400
And they're actually--

00:44:26.400 --> 00:44:28.400
I did work on it, but
they were really the folks

00:44:28.400 --> 00:44:30.510
who did most of the work.

00:44:30.510 --> 00:44:31.770
So thank you.

00:44:31.770 --> 00:44:36.180
So let's sum up by
dispelling a couple of myths.

00:44:36.180 --> 00:44:38.130
I think you'll
sometimes hear that you

00:44:38.130 --> 00:44:41.040
need neural networks in order to
do machine learning these days.

00:44:41.040 --> 00:44:43.060
And I don't think that's true.

00:44:43.060 --> 00:44:44.880
I think, hopefully,
we've shown you today

00:44:44.880 --> 00:44:46.620
that Scikit-Learn
and XGBoost can

00:44:46.620 --> 00:44:49.710
be really helpful in solving
a bunch of business problems

00:44:49.710 --> 00:44:51.390
with ML.

00:44:51.390 --> 00:44:53.290
And if you do have
unstructured data,

00:44:53.290 --> 00:44:56.220
like images or audio files,
and you want to make sense out

00:44:56.220 --> 00:45:00.090
of them, another option, instead
of building your own neural

00:45:00.090 --> 00:45:02.790
network-- or certainly
something to consider--

00:45:02.790 --> 00:45:05.850
is to process them through
our building blocks

00:45:05.850 --> 00:45:08.230
APIs and then take the
structure out of that,

00:45:08.230 --> 00:45:12.072
and then use XGBoost
or Scikit-Learn.

00:45:12.072 --> 00:45:14.280
People may tell you that
you need to leave the Python

00:45:14.280 --> 00:45:16.590
ecosystem to scale.

00:45:16.590 --> 00:45:18.000
I also don't think this is true.

00:45:18.000 --> 00:45:19.710
I think using some
of the techniques

00:45:19.710 --> 00:45:22.770
that we showed you today, you
can scale up on single machines

00:45:22.770 --> 00:45:25.020
and get a ton of
amazing work done

00:45:25.020 --> 00:45:29.610
without having to go to
a distributed framework.

00:45:29.610 --> 00:45:32.970
And people may tell you that
the only way to productionize

00:45:32.970 --> 00:45:35.070
is to embrace containerization.

00:45:35.070 --> 00:45:37.446
And certainly,
containerisation has its place,

00:45:37.446 --> 00:45:38.820
and there is lots
of places where

00:45:38.820 --> 00:45:40.420
it's very, very appropriate.

00:45:40.420 --> 00:45:42.300
But I think for
many folks, the ease

00:45:42.300 --> 00:45:45.270
of use of a managed service
like Cloud ML Engine

00:45:45.270 --> 00:45:46.350
can be very compelling.

00:45:46.350 --> 00:45:54.009
